/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An aircraft carries four main forces: lift L, weight W, thrust T and drag D.
   - The load factor n = L / W is dimensionless.
   - Limit load is the largest load expected in normal service.
   - Lift is not the only load on a wing section.
   - The same inertia loads act on every seat, galley and fitting in the fuselage, and together with the tail load they bend the fuselage, so each frame station is checked with the masses on either side of it.
   - Shear force and bending moment distribution on wings integrates this net load to get shear force and bending moment.
   - Design uses a factor of safety of 1.5 on the limit load.
   - Pult is ultimate load in N; Plim is limit load in N; 1.5 is the aircraft factor of safety, dimensionless.
   - A critical load case is the combination of loads that gives the highest stress in a particular member.
*/
