/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Load factor n is lift divided by weight, n = L/W.
   - W/S is wing loading in N/m²; ρ is air density in kg/m³; CLmax is dimensionless.
   - At a higher speed the same wing can pull more load factor before it stalls.
   - n is load factor (dimensionless); V is true airspeed in m/s; VS1 is the one-g stall speed in m/s.
   - This is the stall line.
   - The structure has a positive limit load factor nmax and a negative limit nmin: for transport aircraft at least 2.5 and −1.0, for light aircraft about 3.8 and −1.5.
   - VA is the manoeuvre speed in m/s; VS1 is the one-g stall speed in m/s; nmax is the positive limit load factor.
   - From VA the top stays flat at nmax all the way to VD, the design dive speed; VC, the design cruising speed, lies between them.
   - A sharp vertical gust of speed U changes the angle of attack by U/V, and the lift-curve slope a (per radian) turns this into extra lift:
   - ρ is air density in kg/m³; V is airspeed in m/s; a is the lift-curve slope per radian; U is the gust velocity in m/s; W/S is the wing loading in N/m².
*/
