/* book Worked example 2: Q: The same aircraft (W/S = 3000 N/m², ρ = 1.225 kg/m³, nmax = 2.5, nmin = −1.0) has VC = 120 m/s and VD = 160 m/s. The lift-curve slope is a = 5.0 per radian. Using sharp-edged gusts of 15 m/s at VC and 7.5 m/s at VD, find the gust load factors and say whether a gust or the manoeuvre limit sizes the wing for upward bending.
   1. With Δn = ρ V a U / (2 W/S) and 2 W/S = 6000 N/m², at VC: Δn = 1.225 × 120 × 5.0 × 15 ÷ 6000 = 11 025 ÷ 6000 = 1.8375. So n = 1 + 1.8375 = 2.84 (upward gust) and n = 1 − 1.8375 = −0.84 (downward gust).
   2. At VD: Δn = 1.225 × 160 × 5.0 × 7.5 ÷ 6000 = 7350 ÷ 6000 = 1.225. So n = 2.23 and n = −0.23.
   3. Only 2.84 lies outside the manoeuvre limits (2.5 and −1.0).
   4. The upward gust at VC is critical: 2.84 ÷ 2.5 = 1.135, so the wing root bending is about 13.5 % higher than for the manoeuvre corner.
   Answer: Gust load factors are 2.84 and −0.84 at VC, 2.23 and −0.23 at VD. The upward gust at VC (n = 2.84) sizes the wing for upward bending, not the manoeuvre limit of 2.5. */
